1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,680
VidyutRakshak: explainable AI for electricity theft detection, built for GUVNL under the Seva First Innovation Challenge.

2
00:00:08,280 --> 00:00:12,664
Meter bypass, tampering and direct hooking hide among lakhs of consumers.

3
00:00:12,664 --> 00:00:19,029
Inspections are picked from bill scrutiny and tip-offs, so only about five in a hundred visits find theft.

4
00:00:19,029 --> 00:00:26,056
And simple rules that flag a big drop in consumption mostly send inspectors to vacant houses and rooftop solar homes.

5
00:00:26,656 --> 00:00:32,415
VidyutRakshak compares every consumer, every day, with peers on the same transformer and tariff.

6
00:00:32,415 --> 00:00:35,535
Then it checks the transformer's own energy balance.

7
00:00:35,535 --> 00:00:37,094
Theft makes energy vanish.

8
00:00:37,094 --> 00:00:38,474
A vacant home does not.

9
00:00:38,474 --> 00:00:43,213
Every alert comes with reasons, and every inspection outcome teaches the model.

10
00:00:43,823 --> 00:00:53,146
It runs on data GUVNL already has: smart-meter reads, billing and transformer meters, on a single CPU server, with open-source software only.

11
00:00:53,757 --> 00:00:58,934
Here is the working console, on a simulated network of 5,000 consumers.

12
00:00:58,934 --> 00:01:04,840
76 high-risk consumers are not yet inspected, with about ₹60,000 a month at risk.

13
00:01:04,840 --> 00:01:10,163
85% of the top alerts are real theft, against under 5% for random checks.

14
00:01:10,791 --> 00:01:11,956
Take the top alert.

15
00:01:11,956 --> 00:01:15,023
Three reasons, each a number an officer can check.

16
00:01:15,023 --> 00:01:19,316
Consumption fell 23% against commercial peers on the same transformer.

17
00:01:19,316 --> 00:01:21,094
Usage is pinned at a ceiling.

18
00:01:21,094 --> 00:01:23,118
And there were two tamper alarms.

19
00:01:23,118 --> 00:01:27,227
The chart shows the metered reading against what the peers suggest.

20
00:01:27,858 --> 00:01:31,906
Field officers can read the same reasons in Gujarati, with one click.

21
00:01:31,906 --> 00:01:37,187
And today's inspection list downloads as a route sheet, grouped by feeder and transformer.

22
00:01:37,792 --> 00:01:39,650
The transformer view.

23
00:01:39,650 --> 00:01:46,993
DT-002's losses rose from 4.3% to almost 15%, and here are the consumers behind it.

24
00:01:47,626 --> 00:01:48,722
How good is it?

25
00:01:48,722 --> 00:01:55,733
A rule that flags any drop above 50% sends inspectors to 53 vacant or solar homes in its top 71.

26
00:01:55,733 --> 00:01:57,924
VidyutRakshak sends them to 6.

27
00:01:57,924 --> 00:02:04,351
Where consumption fell, the transformer balance cuts those wasted visits by another 19%.

28
00:02:04,960 --> 00:02:07,308
And the scores mean what they say.

29
00:02:07,308 --> 00:02:12,211
On a holdout area, 96% of consumers scored 80–100 were really stealing.

30
00:02:12,827 --> 00:02:18,632
When an officer inspects a consumer, the outcome goes into the vigilance log, with the officer's name.

31
00:02:21,327 --> 00:02:22,067
Now retrain.

32
00:02:22,067 --> 00:02:29,216
One new outcome is not enough evidence, so the promotion gate keeps v4 live, and saves the new model as a candidate.

33
00:02:29,827 --> 00:02:37,190
After a field round of 60 inspections, the new model is compared with v4, on outcomes v4 never saw.

34
00:02:37,190 --> 00:02:39,496
It holds up, so it is promoted.

35
00:02:39,496 --> 00:02:42,099
An admin can roll back at any time.

36
00:02:42,727 --> 00:02:51,577
We also tested the meter-data path on real, public theft data: 42,000 consumers from the SGCC dataset.

37
00:02:51,577 --> 00:02:57,130
The top 4% of alerts are 40% thieves, against an 8.5% base rate.

38
00:02:57,730 --> 00:03:05,694
It holds up under stress: theft that began before the data starts, transformers with no meters, and mapping errors.

39
00:03:05,694 --> 00:03:08,394
The hit rate stays between 81% and 86%.

40
00:03:08,394 --> 00:03:11,303
Subtle bypass is the hardest case, at 73%.

41
00:03:11,903 --> 00:03:16,350
And it scales: 1 lakh consumers are scored in 15 seconds on a laptop.

42
00:03:16,950 --> 00:03:22,213
Explainable, in Gujarati, on-premise, with pseudonymised data and no licence fees.

43
00:03:22,213 --> 00:03:26,256
VidyutRakshak is ready for a one-sub-division pilot with GUVNL.

44
00:03:26,856 --> 00:03:27,390
Thank you.

